-- chem/legendre.cub — the Legendre polynomials at every l, by their closed form. The coefficient of η^{l−2m} in P_l is
-- c(l, m) = (−1)^m (2l−2m)! / (2^l m! (l−m)! (l−2m)!), 0 ≤ m ≤ l/2, and its ratios are rational in l and m (from
-- (n+1)! = (n+1)·n!): c(l+1, m)/c(l, m) = (2l+1−2m)/(l+1−2m), c(l−1, m−1)/c(l, m) = −2m/(l−2m+1), c(l, m−1)/c(l, m) =
-- −2m(2l−2m+1)/((l−2m+2)(l−2m+1)). Divided by c(l, m) ≠ 0, Bonnet's recurrence at η^{l+1−2m},
-- (l+1)c(l+1, m) − (2l+1)c(l, m) + l·c(l−1, m−1) = 0, and the Legendre equation at η^k, k = l−2m,
-- (k+2)(k+1)c(l, m−1) + (l(l+1) − k(k+1))c(l, m) = 0, are rational identities in (l, m) whose numerators have degree at
-- most 4 in each, so they hold everywhere when they hold on a 6 × 6 grid off their poles (`leg-grid`); at m = 0 the
-- terms in c(·, −1) are absent and the ratios give 0, as the identities need. Bonnet's top coefficient, where 2m = l+1
-- and P_l has no term: (l+1) + l·c(l−1, m−1)/c(l+1, m) = 0 with that ratio −4m(l+1−m)/((2l+2−2m)(2l+1−2m)) = −(l+1)/l
-- (`leg-top`, a rational identity in l of degree 2, on 4 points). So the closed form satisfies Bonnet's recurrence
-- (hence is chem/separate.cub's P_l, both starting from P_0 = 1, P_1 = η) and the Legendre equation, at every l: the
-- equation chem/laplace.cub checked to l = 24, now for all l.
import "separate.hypercubical"

